const TOPICS=[{"id": "water", "title": "Water & its superpowers", "desc": "How a tiny uneven molecule makes life possible.", "icon": "\u25c9", "group": "Unit 2", "parts": [["Start with the uneven pull", "A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom by polar covalent bonds. Oxygen pulls shared electrons more strongly, so its end is slightly negative (\u03b4\u2212). The hydrogen ends are slightly positive (\u03b4+). These are partial charges: the whole molecule is neutral."], ["From polarity to hydrogen bonds", "The slightly positive hydrogen of one water molecule attracts the slightly negative oxygen of another. That attraction is a hydrogen bond. Individual hydrogen bonds are weaker than covalent bonds, but many together have a big effect."], ["Cohesion, adhesion, and surface tension", "Cohesion means water sticks to water. Adhesion means water sticks to other polar surfaces, such as the walls of narrow tubes in plants. Together these help water move through narrow spaces. Surface tension comes from cohesion at the surface."], ["A stable home for life", "Water absorbs a lot of energy before its temperature changes much. This helps bodies and lakes resist rapid temperature swings. Evaporation removes higher-energy molecules and cools a surface, which is why sweating works."], ["Dissolving and floating", "Water surrounds many ions and polar molecules, allowing them to dissolve. Nonpolar oils do not mix well with water. In ice, hydrogen bonds hold molecules in an open arrangement, making ice less dense than liquid water. Floating ice can insulate water below."]], "trap": "Water is not a universal solvent that dissolves everything. Also, the O\u2013H bonds inside a water molecule are covalent; hydrogen bonds mostly connect different molecules.", "example": "A drop clings to a leaf: adhesion helps it stick to the leaf, and cohesion helps the drop stay together.", "model": "water"}, {"id": "ph", "title": "pH & chemical clues", "desc": "Understand acids, bases, buffers, and lab indicators.", "icon": "\u2301", "group": "Unit 2", "parts": [["What the number means", "pH describes hydrogen ion concentration. At about 25\u00b0C, pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, and above 7 is basic. Acids increase H\u207a in water; bases reduce H\u207a concentration, often by accepting H\u207a or releasing OH\u207b."], ["Each step is tenfold", "pH 3 has ten times the hydrogen ion concentration of pH 4, and 100 times that of pH 5. A lower pH means more H\u207a. A two-unit change is not just twice as much!"], ["Why cells care", "Proteins work best in particular chemical conditions. A large pH change can alter interactions that hold a protein in shape. Buffers resist pH changes by accepting or releasing H\u207a. They do not make every solution neutral."], ["Use evidence, not guesses", "An indicator changes color under particular conditions. Litmus tests acidity or basicity; phenolphthalein is pink in sufficiently basic solutions. Iodine turns blue-black with starch. Heated Benedict\u2019s solution detects reducing sugars such as glucose. Bromothymol blue responds to pH; dissolved CO\u2082 can lower pH, shifting its color toward yellow."]], "trap": "Bromothymol blue responds directly to pH, not uniquely to CO\u2082. A color result supports a conclusion only when you know the test conditions and use controls.", "example": "Compare an unknown food with a glucose positive control and water negative control. Heat all Benedict\u2019s samples the same way before comparing colors.", "model": "ph"}, {"id": "building", "title": "Building & breaking molecules", "desc": "Monomers, polymers, and the role of water.", "icon": "\u2295", "group": "Unit 2", "parts": [["Small pieces, larger structures", "A monomer is a small building block. A polymer is a chain of repeating building blocks. Monosaccharides build polysaccharides; amino acids build polypeptides; nucleotides build DNA and RNA. Lipids are grouped with biological macromolecules, but are not true repeating-unit polymers."], ["Dehydration synthesis: build", "In a simplified condensation reaction, an H from one building block and an OH from another form water. A covalent bond links the building blocks. Think: joining pieces releases water. Cells use enzymes and energy-coupled pathways to assemble molecules."], ["Hydrolysis: break", "Hydro means water; lysis means splitting. Hydrolysis uses water to break a covalent bond, adding H and OH to the resulting pieces. Digestion uses enzyme-catalyzed hydrolysis to turn large food molecules into smaller absorbable molecules."], ["Count connections", "A single unbranched chain of n monomers has n\u22121 connections between monomers. In the simplified dehydration model, joining five separate monomers into one chain releases four water molecules. Breaking all four connections uses four waters."]], "trap": "Water is released during dehydration synthesis and used during hydrolysis. Breaking a bond itself requires energy; do not assume every breakdown reaction automatically releases usable energy.", "example": "A starch chain is too large to absorb directly. Digestive enzymes help hydrolyze it into smaller sugars that can be absorbed.", "model": "building"}, {"id": "molecules", "title": "The four molecule families", "desc": "Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids.", "icon": "\u2b21", "group": "Unit 2", "parts": [["Carbohydrates: fuel and framework", "Simple sugars such as glucose are monosaccharides. Two linked sugars make a disaccharide; many make a polysaccharide. Starch stores glucose in plants, glycogen stores it in animals, and cellulose supports plant cell walls. Many carbohydrates have an approximate 1:2:1 C:H:O ratio, but it is not a universal identification rule."], ["Lipids: storage and membranes", "Fats store lots of energy, help insulate, and cushion organs. A triglyceride contains glycerol and three fatty acids. Phospholipids form the basic structure of cell membranes. Steroids are also lipids. Lipids are largely nonpolar and hydrophobic."], ["Saturated or unsaturated?", "Saturated fatty acid chains have no carbon\u2013carbon double bonds. Cis unsaturated chains have one or more double bonds that create kinks, making tight packing harder. This often makes fats more fluid. Trans double bonds are straighter than cis ones. Partial hydrogenation can create trans fats; adding hydrogen is not the definition of a trans bond."], ["Proteins: the cell\u2019s workers", "Amino acids link into polypeptides that fold into functional proteins. Proteins act as enzymes, transporters, receptors, antibodies, and structural supports. Each amino acid has an amino group, a carboxyl group, a hydrogen, and an R group attached to its central carbon. R groups differ."], ["Nucleic acids: information", "DNA and RNA are polymers of nucleotides. Each nucleotide has a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen-containing base. DNA stores hereditary information; RNA helps express that information, including during protein production. Food is not the only place these molecules exist: every living cell contains biological molecules."]], "trap": "Carbohydrates do more than provide energy, and proteins do more than build muscles. Identify a molecule using its structure and function together.", "example": "A potato stores starch. Your liver stores glycogen. A plant cell wall contains cellulose. All three are carbohydrates, but their structures and jobs differ.", "model": "families"}, {"id": "proteins", "title": "Protein shape \u2192 protein job", "desc": "Follow a chain from amino acids to a working protein.", "icon": "\u3030", "group": "Unit 2", "parts": [["Primary: the sequence", "Primary structure is the order of amino acids in a polypeptide. Covalent peptide bonds join the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid to the amino nitrogen of another. A sequence change can change folding, but does not always change function."], ["Secondary: local patterns", "Some backbone regions form alpha helices or beta sheets. Hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N\u2013H groups stabilize these patterns. These are backbone interactions, not the R-group interactions that define tertiary folding."], ["Tertiary: one chain\u2019s overall fold", "A polypeptide folds into a three-dimensional shape. R-group interactions help stabilize it: hydrogen bonds, ionic attractions, hydrophobic interactions, and covalent disulfide bridges between cysteines. Nonpolar side chains often cluster away from water."], ["Quaternary: a team of chains", "Some functional proteins contain two or more polypeptide subunits. Their arrangement is quaternary structure. Hemoglobin is one example. A protein made from one polypeptide does not have quaternary structure."], ["Denaturation: losing the useful shape", "Heat or extreme pH can disrupt interactions that stabilize a protein\u2019s shape. This can change an active site or other functional region and reduce function. Typical denaturation leaves the peptide backbone and amino acid sequence intact. Some proteins can refold; others cannot under those conditions."]], "trap": "Denaturation usually changes folding, not primary sequence. Hydrophobic interactions are not covalent bonds, and ordinary heating does not necessarily break disulfide bonds.", "example": "Cooking an egg changes how albumin and other proteins fold and interact. The egg white becomes opaque and firm. Cooling it does not simply turn it back into raw egg white.", "model": "protein"}];
const QUESTIONS=[{"topic": "water", "prompt": "Which statement best explains water\u2019s polarity?", "options": ["Oxygen attracts shared electrons more strongly than hydrogen.", "Water has an overall negative charge.", "Hydrogen bonds hold the O and H atoms together inside water.", "Water contains ions instead of atoms."], "answer": 0, "why": "Unequal electron sharing creates partial charges. Water is neutral overall, with polar covalent O\u2013H bonds."}, {"topic": "water", "prompt": "Ice floats. How can this help a pond ecosystem in winter?", "options": ["Ice warms all water to the same temperature.", "Floating ice can insulate liquid water below.", "Ice supplies all the oxygen fish need.", "Freezing removes all dissolved solutes."], "answer": 1, "why": "Ice is less dense because of its open molecular arrangement. A surface layer can reduce heat loss from water below."}, {"topic": "water", "prompt": "Water clinging to a glass tube is an example of\u2026", "options": ["hydrolysis.", "adhesion.", "denaturation.", "a peptide bond."], "answer": 1, "why": "Adhesion is attraction to another material; cohesion is attraction among water molecules."}, {"topic": "ph", "prompt": "Compared with pH 5, pH 3 has\u2026", "options": ["2 times as much H\u207a.", "2 times less H\u207a.", "100 times as much H\u207a.", "100 times less H\u207a."], "answer": 2, "why": "Each decrease of one pH unit is a tenfold increase in H\u207a. Two units means 10 \u00d7 10 = 100."}, {"topic": "ph", "prompt": "Why include water when testing a food with Benedict\u2019s solution?", "options": ["It provides a negative control.", "It guarantees a positive result.", "It changes starch into protein.", "It eliminates the need for heating."], "answer": 0, "why": "A negative control shows what the test looks like without the target substance under the same conditions."}, {"topic": "ph", "prompt": "A buffer helps a cell by\u2026", "options": ["making all solutions pH 7.", "preventing any chemical reactions.", "resisting changes in pH.", "destroying H\u207a permanently."], "answer": 2, "why": "Buffers accept or release H\u207a to reduce pH changes within a range; they do not force pH to 7."}, {"topic": "building", "prompt": "In the simplified model, joining 6 monomers into one unbranched chain releases how many waters?", "options": ["6", "5", "7", "12"], "answer": 1, "why": "Six pieces require five links. Each link formed in the simplified dehydration model releases one water."}, {"topic": "building", "prompt": "A digestive enzyme breaks a polymer by adding water. This is\u2026", "options": ["dehydration synthesis.", "hydrolysis.", "photosynthesis.", "diffusion."], "answer": 1, "why": "Hydrolysis uses water to split a bond. Dehydration synthesis joins building blocks and releases water."}, {"topic": "building", "prompt": "Which family is not a true repeating-monomer polymer?", "options": ["Proteins", "DNA", "Polysaccharides", "Lipids"], "answer": 3, "why": "Lipids include structures such as triglycerides and steroids, rather than long repeating-monomer chains."}, {"topic": "molecules", "prompt": "Which pairing is correct?", "options": ["Cellulose\u2014animal energy storage", "Glycogen\u2014animal glucose storage", "Starch\u2014genetic information", "DNA\u2014fatty acid storage"], "answer": 1, "why": "Glycogen stores glucose in animals. Starch stores glucose in plants; cellulose supports plant cell walls."}, {"topic": "molecules", "prompt": "A molecule contains glycerol joined to three fatty acids. It is a\u2026", "options": ["polypeptide.", "nucleotide.", "triglyceride.", "monosaccharide."], "answer": 2, "why": "A triglyceride is a lipid made from glycerol and three fatty acids."}, {"topic": "molecules", "prompt": "Cis unsaturated fatty acid chains often make a fat more fluid because\u2026", "options": ["they form peptide bonds.", "their kinks interfere with tight packing.", "they have no carbon.", "they contain more DNA."], "answer": 1, "why": "Cis double bonds introduce bends, preventing the close packing typical of straighter chains."}, {"topic": "proteins", "prompt": "Which protein level is the amino acid sequence?", "options": ["Primary", "Secondary", "Tertiary", "Quaternary"], "answer": 0, "why": "Primary is sequence. Secondary is local helices/sheets, tertiary is one chain\u2019s fold, and quaternary is multiple subunits."}, {"topic": "proteins", "prompt": "After typical heat denaturation, what usually remains intact?", "options": ["The original active-site shape", "All hydrogen bonds", "The amino acid sequence", "All interactions among subunits"], "answer": 2, "why": "Typical denaturation disrupts folding while leaving most peptide bonds and thus primary sequence intact."}, {"topic": "proteins", "prompt": "What mainly stabilizes alpha helices and beta sheets?", "options": ["Hydrogen bonds between backbone groups", "Bonds between all R groups", "Glycosidic bonds", "New nucleotides"], "answer": 0, "why": "Secondary structure relies on backbone C=O and N\u2013H hydrogen bonds."}, {"topic": "proteins", "prompt": "Two cysteine side chains can form a\u2026", "options": ["hydrogen ion.", "disulfide bridge.", "nucleotide.", "starch chain."], "answer": 1, "why": "Cysteine side chains can form a covalent disulfide bridge that stabilizes a folded protein."}];
